# RFC 6191: the stack closed first and holds the four-tuple in TIME_WAIT. A
# new SYN on it whose TSval is newer than TS.Recent is provably not an old
# duplicate, so it is accepted at once (as Linux does) instead of drawing
# challenge ACKs until 2*MSL runs out. The old connection is closed and
# reaped; a brand-new one answers, its ISN at least 65537 past the old
# SND.NXT (2 here: SYN and FIN) so nothing of the old one can land in its
# window. The new connection carries nothing over: its first ACK and data
# are checked against its own TS.Recent, not the old one.
0      listen 8080
+0     < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,TS val 100 ecr 0>
+0     > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 <mss 1460,TS val * ecr 100>
+0.005 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 105 ecr 0>
+0     accept

+0.010 close
+0     > F. 1:1(0) ack 1 <...>
+0.005 < . 1:1(0) ack 2 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 120 ecr 0>
+0     state FIN_WAIT_2
+0.005 < F. 1:1(0) ack 2 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 130 ecr 0>
+0     > . 2:2(0) ack 2 <...>
+0     state TIME_WAIT

+1     expect_new_isn 65539
+0     < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,TS val 1130 ecr 0>
+0     > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 <mss 1460,TS val * ecr 1130>
+0     state CLOSED
+0.005 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 1135 ecr 0>
+0     accept
+0     state ESTABLISHED
+0.005 < P. 1:11(10) ack 1 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 1140 ecr 0>
+0.200 > . 1:1(0) ack 11 <...>
+0     read 10
